The hero download CTA had multiple overlapping variables (`heroInfo`, `heroDownloadUrl`, `heroDownloadIsStore`, `storeLabel`) duplicating the same href/label data. The Microsoft Store icon in the Windows download list also used an undefined CSS class.

## Changes

- **`downloads.ts`**: Replace the four hero-CTA variables with a single `HeroCta` interface (`href`, `label`, `icon`, `isExternal`, `meta`). Windows path constructs the Store CTA directly—independent of `bestAsset`—so the Store button renders even when no GitHub release assets are found.

```typescript
interface HeroCta {
  href: string
  label: string
  icon: string
  isExternal: boolean
  meta?: string  // e.g. "v1.2.3 · 94 MB" — only for native-asset CTAs
}

const heroCta: HeroCta | null = (() => {
  if (currentOS === 'windows') {
    return { href: MICROSOFT_STORE_URL, label: 'Microsoft Store から入手', icon: iconMicrosoftStore, isExternal: true }
  }
  if (!bestAsset) return null
  const { icon, label } = heroButtonInfo(bestAsset)
  return { href: bestAsset.url, label, icon, isExternal: false, meta: `v${version} · ${bestAsset.size}` }
})()
```

- **`downloads.ts` (store list item)**: Replace undefined `download-item-icon` class with the existing `.platform-icon` class for consistent icon sizing.

